## 1. Reality Baseline: The Hole We Are Standing In

Before any transformation, the situation must be named without euphemism.

**Advertiser Fragility.** US ad revenue has declined at least 55% year-over-year every month since the 2022 acquisition, with a peak drop of 78% in December 2022. Recovery has begun — Q1 2026 ad revenue reached approximately $750 million, up from approximately $530 million year-over-year, with full-year 2025 around $2.9 billion — but this remains approximately 35% below pre-acquisition peaks. This stabilization is sentiment-driven, not structural. Brand safety and content adjacency guarantees remain absent. Recovery can reverse in a single quarter.

**Bot Epidemic.** An estimated 50 to 84 million active accounts are synthetic, representing 9 to 15% of accounts overall and up to 44% of participants in high-engagement categories such as politics and entertainment. Ongoing 2026 purges are removing AI-powered bots at scale, but reactive purges are not a permanent defense. Bots are not a moderation nuisance. They are an AI contamination problem. A model trained on this data will learn from and amplify synthetic signals, making the AI-native thesis self-defeating at the infrastructure level before it begins.

**Regulatory Pressure.** The EU AI Act's high-risk obligations face procedural delays, but transparency requirements under Article 50 apply now. Ofcom, the ICO, and the European Commission opened active investigations in early 2026 into Grok-generated deepfakes, including non-consensual nudification and CSAM-adjacent content. These are not hypothetical future risks. They are active investigations with enforcement capacity, and they predate any AI-native roadmap.

**User Flight.** Bluesky has grown to approximately 44 million registered users, driven substantially by journalists, civil-society organizations, and professional creators leaving X. This exodus is not driven by feature envy. It is driven by a perceived governance vacuum. Bluesky's decentralized AT Protocol and published transparency report — documenting 3.5 million user reports and 114 government legal requests processed — are structural answers to the accountability absence users perceive here. A meaningful user cohort is choosing governance over scale. That is a signal worth taking seriously.

The consequence of ignoring this baseline is straightforward: an AI-native platform built on unresolved bot contamination, collapsed advertiser trust, active regulatory investigations, and departing users would amplify every one of those problems. The first task is to repair the substrate.

