## 5. Human Verification at Scale: Tiered Identity with eIDAS 2.0

Mandatory government ID verification for all users is legally fraught under GDPR, technically unrealistic at 500 million-plus scale, and politically unacceptable to the user base. The solution is a tiered, incentive-aligned system where verification is rewarded, not required.

Tier 1 is anonymous. Users can view and post, but content is ineligible for the Verified Human Layer and carries an unverified signal in the platform's internal risk scoring.

Tier 2 is phone-verified via one-time passcode. Content becomes eligible for the Verified Human Layer with a lower algorithmic weight than Tier 3, and the account carries a moderate trust signal internally.

Tier 3 is identity-verified via government-grade proofing. In the EU, this means integration with the EU Digital Identity Wallet under eIDAS 2.0. Outside the EU, comparable pseudonymous token systems from certified third-party providers serve the same function. These accounts receive the highest algorithmic priority, a human verification badge, higher ad revenue share, exclusive tools, and better ad content adjacency. They form the primary training source for Grok's learning corpus.

The eIDAS 2.0 integration is the most significant technical upgrade in this proposal. Under Regulation EU 2024/1183, all 27 EU Member States must provide citizens with European Digital Identity Wallets by December 2026, with mandatory private-sector acceptance beginning December 2027. These wallets use Zero-Knowledge Proofs that allow verification of attributes — such as "authenticated human, over 18" — without exposing underlying identity data to the platform. X receives an attestation. It never receives raw ID information. This satisfies GDPR's data minimization principle by design, not by workaround.

No major social platform has committed to integrating eIDAS wallets for content authenticity purposes. This is a defensible first-mover claim that C2PA adoption alone cannot provide. It turns the verification infrastructure into a regulatory advantage rather than a privacy liability.

Universal adoption is not required. Even 5 to 10% of active users — 25 to 50 million accounts — operating at Tier 3 provides a signal layer sufficient to anchor Grok's learning, create a monetizable verified attention pool, and demonstrate to regulators that X has built a functional, scalable authenticity infrastructure.

