## 6. Content Provenance: The Three-Layer C2PA Durable Credential System

C2PA metadata alone is insufficient. Every major social platform, including X, strips embedded file metadata during the upload pipeline as a standard processing step. Screenshots destroy manifest data entirely. File conversion eliminates it. The EU AI Act's Code of Practice explicitly acknowledges this limitation and mandates a multi-layer approach for exactly this reason. A commitment to C2PA without the full three-layer system is a commitment to an approach that fails the moment any content leaves its native environment.

The three layers function as an integrated system. Pillar 1 is the Hard Binding — a cryptographically signed C2PA manifest embedded in the file container. It is the authoritative, tamper-evident record of provenance. It is also the layer that metadata stripping destroys, which is why it cannot stand alone.

Pillar 2 is the Soft Binding — an imperceptible identifier embedded in the image's pixel data, not its file header, that points to the full manifest stored in a cloud-based C2PA Manifest Store. Because it lives in the pixels rather than the container, it survives the recompression and format conversion that destroys the manifest. The C2PA 2.1 specification formally supports this recovery mechanism.

Pillar 3 is Perceptual Fingerprinting — a content-based hash derived from the image's visual features, stable across compression and resizing. It provides a second recovery path and, critically, prevents a valid watermark from being copied from one image to another. Without the fingerprint, spoofing attacks are trivial.

Without the manifest, there is no authoritative signed record. Without the watermark, a stripped image has no recovery path. Without the fingerprint, the entire system is vulnerable to spoofing. All three are required.

Deepfake response must be operationally specified. High-severity deepfake content — non-consensual nudification, CSAM-adjacent material, coordinated political harm — will be escalated to human operators within minutes, with public transparency logs on detection and removal rates.

A Certificate Revocation and Continuity Plan is mandatory, not optional. In January 2025, Nikon was forced to revoke all C2PA certificates issued by its Z6 III camera after a signing vulnerability was demonstrated, invalidating every credential those cameras had produced. This is the defining cautionary case for C2PA infrastructure at scale. X will implement real-time certificate revocation list checking, automated re-issuance protocols for compromised certificates, public transparency logging of all certificate lifecycle events, and quarterly third-party security audits of the signing infrastructure. Provenance infrastructure that has no continuity plan is a single incident away from total failure.

