## 8. Regulatory Compliance: Use the Breathing Room, Don't Squander It

The EU AI Act's high-risk obligations have encountered procedural delays. This is not a reprieve. Ofcom, the ICO, and the European Commission have active investigations with enforcement capacity that do not wait for legislative timelines. The appropriate response to procedural breathing room is accelerated voluntary compliance, not deferred action.

Phase 0a runs from now through July 2026. The required actions are: submit a structured preliminary conformity assessment to EU authorities outlining Grok's risk management system, human oversight mechanisms, technical documentation framework, and audit trail architecture; engage proactively with Ofcom and EU investigators and disclose the dual-layer content architecture, the C2PA three-layer deployment plan, the eIDAS 2.0 integration roadmap, and the governance workforce scaling timeline; halt public-facing Grok-assisted post composition, image generation, and video generation where outputs lack automatic C2PA manifest and a user-visible AI indicator — internal experimentation continues under compliance review — this is the specific scope, not a blanket shutdown; publish a public AI Governance Commitment that maps X's architecture onto the EU AI Act's specific articles with a committed third-party audit schedule; and deploy a public bot and verified-content dashboard showing synthetic account removal rates, verified layer penetration, and C2PA credential coverage, updated daily or weekly.

Phase 0b runs from August 2026 through Q2 2027. This phase completes the full conformity assessment and implements all required risk mitigations, deploys the three-layer C2PA durable credential system for all AI-generated content, pilots eIDAS wallet integration in three EU Member States, passes external audit for high-risk AI obligations, and submits a binding remediation schedule to the EU Commission for any outstanding items.

Good-faith engagement before enforcement is qualitatively different from post-penalty scrambling. Regulators distinguish between platforms that come to them with a plan and platforms that respond only when compelled.

